\import{fnip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fnip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The time interval may be finite}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{The constant #{c} in \ref{fnip-0029} depends on the point and on
the [vector field](fnip-0009). A solution may leave #{U} at a finite
time. The same page of Guckenheimer and Holmes gives the scalar
equation
##{\dot x=1+x^{2},}
whose solutions are #{x(t)=\tan(t+c)}. Each solution becomes
unbounded at a finite time, so the interval in the theorem is a
proper open interval even when #{U=\mathbb{R}}.}

\p{\citet{(1.0.5), §1.0, p. 3}{guckenheimer1983nonlinear}
is that equation.
\citet{Theorem 1.4, §1.4, p. 22}{kuznetsov2004elements}
states the alternative: the orbit leaves #{U} at an endpoint of its
interval, or the interval is the whole line.}
